---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="RESC containers"
  description="A second container format used by a small set of files. Similar to RESO but with named entries."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'File Formats', href: `${base}/field-guide/file-formats/` },
    { label: 'RESC' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    A handful of files use a different container format from
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/file-formats/reso/`}>RESO</a>. It looks
    the same on the surface — same kind of header, same idea of
    entries pointing at chunks — but the entries are larger and they
    carry names.
  </p>

  <p>
    We see RESC in two places in the NDS version:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>item/itemslot.lzs</code> and <code>item/ItemEtc.lzs</code> —
      item-slot metadata.</li>
    <li>About 70 compressed files, mostly under
      <code>data/2d/solvedeventlist/</code>, plus a few odd ones
      (<code>data/goodslist.lzs</code>, <code>data/mailorder.lzs</code>).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The header</h2>
  <p>
    Same shape as RESO. Magic, file count, version, reserved word.
    Version is always <code>3.11</code> in the files we've seen.
  </p>

  <pre><code>{`+0x00  4 bytes  "RESC"  magic
+0x04  u32      file_count
+0x08  4 bytes  version  ("3.11")
+0x0c  u32      reserved (zeros)`}</code></pre>

  <h2>The entries</h2>
  <p>
    Each entry is 48 bytes. The big difference from RESO is the
    embedded names — two 16-byte fields giving the entry's identifier
    and type, both stored as reversed ASCII (the Konami convention).
  </p>

  <pre><code>{`+0x00  16 bytes  name1 (reversed ASCII)     e.g. "u_gak_noci" → "icon_kag_u"
+0x10  16 bytes  name2 (reversed ASCII)     e.g. "rgcn" → "NCGR"
+0x20  u32       unknown (usually 0)
+0x24  4 bytes   type tag (reversed ASCII)
+0x28  u32       length
+0x2C  u32       offset (from start of file)`}</code></pre>

  <h2>How much we've verified</h2>
  <p>
    The 48-byte stride and the field layout match across the two RESC
    files we've parsed in depth (<code>uma00.lzs</code> and
    <code>goodslist.lzs</code>), but we haven't exhaustively verified
    it on every RESC in the game. There's also an additional four bytes
    at the end of each entry whose purpose isn't certain — possibly a
    checksum or a flag word. Don't trust the layout for every file
    without checking.
  </p>

  <p>
    For palette-association scans across the whole game, we treat RESC
    entries as if they were RESO entries (ignoring the name fields and
    just reading the type tag, length, and offset). This works because
    everything we need to render an image is in those three fields.
  </p>
</FieldGuideLayout>
